  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find all primes $p$ for which $x^2+x-p=0$ has rational roots.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$p=2$ only.\n\nThe Integer Root Theorem restricts possible rational roots to $\\pm1,\\pm p$.",
      "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^2+x-p$. By the Integer Root Theorem, any rational root is an integer divisor of $p$. Since $p$ is prime, the only candidates are $1,-1,p,-p$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1+1-p\\\\P(1)&=2-p\\\\P(-1)&=1-1-p\\\\P(-1)&=-p\\\\P(p)&=p^2+p-p\\\\P(p)&=p^2\\\\P(-p)&=p^2-p-p\\\\P(-p)&=p(p-2)\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $p\\ge2$, these evaluations can be zero only when $p=2$. For this value,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+x-2&=(x-1)(x+2).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe roots are $1$ and $-2$, both rational. Therefore, $p=2$ is the only prime.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
